  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 103 AM EDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nCurrently, a large and broad upper low is anchored over the Hudson \nBay while an upper level high is centered over the four corners \narea. Quasi-zonal flow aloft over the Rockies has initiated lee \ncyclogenesis over the Dakotas. The southerly LLJ is continuing to \nreturn deep Gulf moisture into the central plains this morning, \nafter a brief push back to the southeast post cold front a couple of \ndays ago. Near the surface, ridging continues over MI this morning \nbut will be exiting near sunrise. Southwest winds will quickly \nbecome lightly gusty over parts of northern MI as the gradients \ntighten today. \n\nThe drier CAN airmass, which settled in yesterday, has resulted in \nTds in the 50s over MI, reaching into WI as well. As winds turn \nsouthwest today, it will take some time for the deeper moisture \nbehind the warm front to reach all the way into northern MI (if\nat all). Although this is the case, chances for scattered \nshowers and storms exist today due to convective initiation to \nthe west, and steering flow moving it towards MI. Depending on \nhow storms strengthen and organize (MCS?), the track of the \nsteering winds and instability gradient will likely take them \ninto south and central MI around midday/early afternoon. Storms\nare already initiating over the Dakotas (which verifies a few \nCAMs on location and timing). If storms can organize and \nstrengthen, gusty outflow winds will be possible for the Lk MI \nshore communities south of M-72 (up to 40 mph gusts). The main \nhazards, however, will be lightning and brief heavy rain. Any \ntraining of storms could result in flooding of lower lying \nareas. \n\nAfter this, the surface low will usher in behind the convection this \nevening. CAMs rope off the more plentiful moisture to the south, \nwhich will result in little to no instability while the surface low \nmeanders overhead. Although this set up would struggle to generate \nshowers and storms, a slight chance remains for showers this evening \nand tonight due to the chance of lingering moisture (especailly with \nthe absence of a drier airmass scouring things out). More optimistic \nsoundings show shallow instability, resulting in the favoring of \nshowers overnight vs storms. Very low chances for hazards with\nthese storms besides a handful of lightning strikes. \n\nLight north winds signal the very gradual movement of surface high \npressure seeping into northern MI Monday. By the afternoon, the best \ninstability will reside over the southern parts of the state.\nWith that said, PWATs of 1\" or more remain over the northern\npart of the state, with around a 1000 j/kg of SB instability \nover during the afternoon. Showers and thunderstorms are likely\nto form along lake breeze boundaries (which will be mostly \ndiurnally driven). Enough shear will be present for the \nopportunity of a few popcorn convective cells to grow. \n\nAnother possible surge of the warm and hot airmass to the south \npresents itself on Tuesday, before a larger CAN surface high will \nsettle over the state for the weekend. This could drop temperatures \ninto the high 60s to low 70s in some spots.",
